# docflow — environment defaults
#
# Copy to `.env` and adjust. Values here are defaults for every invocation;
# a CLI flag always overrides the matching variable.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# `.env` is git-ignored. Never commit real credentials.
#
# This file covers the first instance: the **operational** settings only —
# paths, slots, model and host. Corpus policy is not here; see the end of the
# file for why. Deferred flags are not here either — see `03-cli.md`, *Options*.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Models
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Local extraction model, used by every pipeline that calls one — EpVR and
# ErpVR, including M4-EpVR, whose extractor reads pixels directly. A
# per-invocation --model overrides it, so M4 can point at a vision model
# without changing this.
DOCFLOW_MODEL=ollama:qwen2.5

# The frontier LLM that governs escalation and sets "could not" — see
# 02-components.md. One of: claude | deepseek | openai
DOCFLOW_VALIDATOR=claude

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Provider configuration
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# No CLI flag for these, on purpose: an endpoint and a credential are
# environment facts, not per-invocation decisions. Keeping them here means
# secrets never reach shell history or a process listing.

# Ollama endpoint. Unset uses the provider default. This is the name K5 reads
# (`DOCFLOW_OLLAMA_HOST` in `docflow/adapters/ollama.py`).
# DOCFLOW_OLLAMA_HOST=http://127.0.0.1:11434

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Ollama sampling options (K5, `llm.local`)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The port's `structured(model, prompt, schema)` takes NO options parameter, so
# the environment is the only channel a caller has. The adapter reads these at
# CALL TIME (`docflow/adapters/ollama.py::_options_from_environment`), never at
# construction — which is why exporting them works even for a client that was
# built before the export.
#
# These are **operational** settings in `NFR-06`'s sense — how a model is asked
# — not corpus policy. Policy lives in `registry/policies/`, because a value
# that changes output without entering the registry hash would let a ledger say
# `done` about a result produced under a setting nothing recorded (ADR-009).
#
# Any other DOCFLOW_OLLAMA_* name is DROPPED, not refused: the runtime owns its
# own option vocabulary, and refusing an unknown name would couple this adapter
# to whatever the runtime adds next.
#
# Value shape: a decimal point makes it a float, otherwise an int.
#   DOCFLOW_OLLAMA_TEMPERATURE=0.2   -> 0.2
#   DOCFLOW_OLLAMA_NUM_CTX=8192      -> 8192
# A malformed value (`abc`, empty) is IGNORED — the option is simply not sent.
# There is no range validation: a negative number is forwarded as-is.

# The context window, in tokens. This is the one that matters most.
#
# The flow declares 8192 itself (`flow/sampling.py::apply_sampling`), so this
# line is usually redundant. It is here because the precedence is
# **environment → file → default**: `apply_sampling` writes only when the
# variable is unset, so a value exported in the shell wins over the flow's own
# declaration, and a value written here does NOT.
#
# Left to the runtime the window is 4096 — measured, `deepseek-r1:1.5b` declares
# 131072 but LOADS at 4096 when nobody asks. That is not a neutral default: a
# `deepseek-r1` step spends part of the window on reasoning tokens before it
# emits a field, and the measured failure is `done_reason: length` with the
# step's fields lost whole.
# DOCFLOW_OLLAMA_NUM_CTX=8192

# Ceiling on generated tokens. Unset lets the runtime decide. Set it low to
# provoke `truncated_output` on purpose — measured, 8 cuts a real answer, and a
# cut is reported rather than parsed because a completion severed after the last
# complete field parses cleanly.
# DOCFLOW_OLLAMA_NUM_PREDICT=

# Sampling parameters. Unset means the runtime's own default applies — this
# adapter never invents a sampling behaviour the caller did not ask for.
# DOCFLOW_OLLAMA_TEMPERATURE=
# DOCFLOW_OLLAMA_TOP_P=
# DOCFLOW_OLLAMA_TOP_K=

# Fixes the sampler's seed, which makes a run reproducible. Useful to tell a real
# value from sampling noise: a field that changes between runs is not being read
# from the document.
# DOCFLOW_OLLAMA_SEED=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Frontier provider (K6)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# These *are* the names the adapter reads (`docflow/adapters/frontier_providers.py`).
# The list is deliberately not the providers' own SDK conventions
# (`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` and friends): K6 takes the key off the environment itself and
# never through a provider SDK, so a `.env` written with the SDK's name would look
# configured and refuse every call.
#
# There are two levels. The shared `DOCFLOW_FRONTIER_KEY` below covers the ordinary
# single-provider setup. A provider-specific name overrides it, and exists because
# `judge` must not be the model that produced the samples: a run wanting two
# *frontier* models needs two keys held at once, which one shared name cannot express.
#
# Precedence is `provider-specific → shared → no key`.
#
# These are secrets — never in a committed file.
# DOCFLOW_FRONTIER_KEY=
# DOCFLOW_FRONTIER_ANTHROPIC_KEY=
# DOCFLOW_FRONTIER_DEEPSEEK_KEY=
# DOCFLOW_FRONTIER_OPENAI_KEY=

# Base URL overrides, one per provider. Unset uses the provider's default, which is
# already correct in every case below.
#
#   anthropic  https://api.anthropic.com
#   deepseek   https://api.deepseek.com/anthropic   <- the /anthropic suffix is
#              load-bearing: DeepSeek's bare host serves the OpenAI dialect, and only
#              this path serves the Anthropic one the adapter speaks
#   openai     https://api.openai.com
# DOCFLOW_FRONTIER_ANTHROPIC_HOST=
# DOCFLOW_FRONTIER_DEEPSEEK_HOST=
# DOCFLOW_FRONTIER_OPENAI_HOST=

# The response ceiling K6 buys, in tokens. Required rather than defaulted: the
# ceiling is a policy decision, and `frontier.py::_max_tokens` refuses without it
# instead of choosing how much answer the caller pays for.
#
# **Size it for the provider's reasoning.** Measured on `deepseek-v4-pro`: a one-page
# invoice spent 7 743 tokens *completing* to emit a two-field object, because the
# model reasons before it answers. A ceiling of 2048 truncates that call, and
# truncation is reported as `truncated_output` rather than parsed.
# DOCFLOW_FRONTIER_MAX_TOKENS=8192

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Paths
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Output directory. Results, ledgers and intermediates all live here, and a
# folder input mirrors its tree. See "Output directory" in 03-cli.md.
DOCFLOW_OUT=out

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Execution
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Concurrency for batch runs. Size to the machine, not to the corpus: the
# local model is usually the bottleneck, not the CPU.
DOCFLOW_JOBS=2

# Pipeline used when neither --pipeline nor --extractor is given.
# The thirteen codes are in 01-pipelines.md.
# DOCFLOW_PIPELINE=M1-ErpVR

# Extractor mode when the material is inferred per file: r | p | rp
# DOCFLOW_EXTRACTOR=rp

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Corpus policy — NOT settings, and deliberately absent from this file
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# These five describe a *corpus*: how strict the quality gate is, how much
# tolerance amounts get. They used to live here as DOCFLOW_* variables. They
# do not any more, and the reason is not tidiness.
#
# The registry hash is a term of the cache key (sad.md §5), which only works
# if the registry is the *sole* source of every value that hash covers. A
# threshold that arrived from the environment would change a stage's output
# without ever entering the key: the ledger would say `done` about a result
# produced under a setting nothing recorded. So policy moved into K8, where the
# hash covers it, and the change now invalidates exactly the stages that read
# it (ADR-009).
#
#   registry/policies/thresholds.yaml
#     segmenter.cut_confidence     was DOCFLOW_CUT_CONFIDENCE=0.7
#     diagnosis.min_chars          was DOCFLOW_MIN_CHARS=100
#     diagnosis.min_dpi            was DOCFLOW_MIN_DPI=200
#     reader.correct               was DOCFLOW_CORRECT=false
#     consistency.tolerance_amounts was DOCFLOW_TOLERANCE_AMOUNTS=1
#
# There is no environment variable and no CLI flag for any of them, at any
# stage. A test asserts it.

# The OCR engine is deliberately absent too: it is Docling, fixed, and not a
# setting. Diagnosis picks the path (conversion or OCR); it never picks the
# engine. A native text PDF is read with pdftotext and Docling does not apply to
# it, and an image PDF is rasterized before Docling sees it.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Notes
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Precedence, highest first:
#
#   1. CLI flag          --jobs 4
#   2. environment       DOCFLOW_JOBS=4
#   3. this file         DOCFLOW_JOBS=2
#   4. built-in default
#
# Not settable, on purpose. A default for any of these would change what a run
# does without anyone asking:
#
#   --force              would reprocess done work on every run
#   --stage              only meaningful alongside --force
#   --only               a persisted scope would narrow every later run
#
# Verification is not configurable either. Every `done` stage is checked
# against the filesystem whenever a ledger is read, because the run that most
# needs the check — the one after a forced kill — is the one least likely to
# ask for it.
